    "created_at": "2015-09-02T13:03:02Z",
    "updated_at": "2015-09-02T13:03:02Z",
    "body": "> > Couldn't a validator give useful guidance on invalid elements/attributes that aren't mentioned in the spec at all?\n> \n> Yes, but validators would have to obtain that information independently\n\nBingo. For all classes of messages the validator emits, we basically need to tie them to citable language/requirements in the spec (including requirements on authors).\n\nIn the case of [the particular section under discussion here](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/obsolete.html#non-conforming-features), the spec not only usefully identifies these elements and attributes as obsolete things (that some documents used in the past), it also identifies features that documents/authors should use in place of them; e.g., for `isindex`, it says, “Use an explicit `form` and _text field_ combination instead.”\n\nSo this section’s useful for the validator as a means to give better help to authors, by providing:\n- an authoritative list of which elements+attributes to give authors special messages for (different from what the validator emits for, say, an element the author just misspelled by mistake)—a message that says basically, “This element was used in some places before but it’s good to use it any longer.”\n- authoritative recommendations an alternative language features that authors should use in place of the obsolete ones\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
