    "created_at": "2015-09-02T13:24:11Z",
    "updated_at": "2015-09-02T13:24:11Z",
    "body": "> Well, I guess I it's fine to include attributes in the spec even when it's only for the benefit of validators, but I don't really understand why validators wouldn't try to be maximally useful even where the spec says nothing.\n\nThey do of course (well the one I work on does at least). Part of the intended value is a (perhaps silly idea) that someday we might have more validators and more people writing validators, and in that case we’d like to have some level of interoperability with regard to what the validators report to users, and the spec provides the basis for that.\n\nAnyway, you could say _I don't really understand why **UAs** wouldn't try to be maximally useful even where the spec says nothing_ and we’d still essentially be talking about the same thing: There are cases where the spec doesn’t necessarily need to say something about what UAs should do but it does anyway because it’s useful to some consistency if we can get it. This isn’t dissimilar from the thinking behind https://github.com/whatwg/html/pull/51.\n",
    "author_association": "MEMBER",
    "pin": null,
