    "created_at": "2015-09-02T15:15:27Z",
    "updated_at": "2015-09-02T15:15:27Z",
    "body": "> So just to be clear, the idea is that implementations do nothing with framespacing\n\nYes.\n\n> but that it's mentioned in the spec for the benefit of validators?\n\nFor the benefit of **authors**, ultimately. “Validators”—which I’d really rather we all just make a habit of calling “HTML checkers” are just tools for helping authors. Mainly they’re for helping author catch unintended mistakes, and for giving authors good useful messages to do that, as well as trying, when possible, to give authors some minimal guidance on how to fix problems.\n\nHTML checkers are just a special class of static-analysis tool/linter; but instead of being based on some arbitrary linting rules, they’re based on standard rules that are documented in the HTML spec.\n\nAnd the goals of all these document-conformance rules in the spec (the requirements that aren’t UA requirements) is also just to help author avoid certain kinds of potential problems that might run into with their HTML documents:  Potential problems for accessibility, usability, interoperability, security, or maintainability. Or that can result in poor performance, or that might cause scripts to fail in ways that are hard to troubleshoot. Or that can run into potential problems in HTML parsing and error-handling behavior so that they end up with some unintuitive, unexpected result in the DOM. And so on.\n\nThe spec has a [**Conformance requirements for authors**](https://html.spec.whatwg.org/multipage/introduction.html#conformance-requirements-for-authors) section that goes into detail about all this stuff. It’s a really great section. Everybody should read it.\n",
